### What is CountDownLatch?

CountDownLatch is a one-shot synchronization aid initialized with a count. `await()` blocks the caller until the count reaches zero. `countDown()` decrements the count by one; it never blocks and is safe to call after the count reaches zero.

### How does the startup gate pattern work?

Every worker calls `startGate.await()` at the top. All block. The coordinator calls `countDown()` once to fire the starting gun, releasing all workers simultaneously.

```java
package com.bank.sync;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class StartupGateDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        CountDownLatch startGate = new CountDownLatch(1);
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    startGate.await();  // all 10 block here
                    System.out.println("Worker-" + id + " starting settlement");
                    doSettlement(id);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
                return null;
            });
        }

        System.out.println("Preparing resources...");
        Thread.sleep(1000);  // setup phase
        startGate.countDown();  // fire the starting gun: all 10 begin together
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### How does the completion gate pattern work?

The count equals the number of workers. Each worker counts down in a finally block when it finishes.

```java
package com.bank.sync;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CompletionGateDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        int workerCount = 10;
        CountDownLatch doneGate = new CountDownLatch(workerCount);
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(workerCount);

        for (int i = 0; i < workerCount; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    doSettlement(id);
                } finally {
                    doneGate.countDown();  // ALWAYS in finally!
                }
            });
        }

        boolean allDone = doneGate.await(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        if (allDone) {
            System.out.println("All settlements complete.");
        } else {
            System.err.println("Timeout: not all workers finished.");
        }
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### Why must countDown be in a finally block?

If a worker throws an exception before decrementing, the coordinator waits forever (or until its own timeout). Putting countDown in a finally block ensures the count is decremented even if the worker crashes.

### How do you prevent infinite waiting?

Always use the timed variant of await. Without a timeout, a crashed worker with a missed countDown causes the coordinator to hang indefinitely.

```java
// WRONG: may wait forever
doneGate.await();

// CORRECT: bounded wait
boolean allDone = doneGate.await(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
if (!allDone) {
    System.err.println("Timeout: " + doneGate.getCount() + " workers still running");
}
```

### Can you combine startup and completion gates?

Yes. Use a startup gate (count 1) to release workers simultaneously, and a completion gate (count N) to wait for all workers to finish. This is the standard pattern for timing a concurrent test.

```java
CountDownLatch startGate = new CountDownLatch(1);
CountDownLatch doneGate = new CountDownLatch(10);

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    pool.submit(() -> {
        try {
            startGate.await();     // wait for the gun
            doSettlement();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        } finally {
            doneGate.countDown();  // signal done
        }
    });
}

long start = System.nanoTime();
startGate.countDown();            // fire the gun
doneGate.await(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);  // wait for all
long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
System.out.println("All done in " + elapsed / 1_000_000 + " ms");
```

### Why can a latch not be reset?

Once the count hits zero it stays zero forever. The countDown calls are idempotent after reaching zero. For repeated rendezvous, use `CyclicBarrier` (automatic reset after each trip) or `Phaser` (dynamic registration and deregistration of parties).

| Feature | CountDownLatch | CyclicBarrier | Phaser |
|---------|---------------|---------------|--------|
| Reset | No | Automatic | Automatic |
| Dynamic parties | No | No | Yes |
| Action on trip | No | Optional Runnable | Yes |
| Use case | One-time event | Repeated rounds | Dynamic phases |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can multiple threads call countDown on the same latch? Yes, any thread can call countDown. Each call decrements by one.
- What happens if you call countDown more times than the initial count? Nothing. Extra calls have no effect once the count reaches zero.
- How does CyclicBarrier handle a broken barrier? If any party throws an exception or times out, the barrier breaks and all waiting parties receive BrokenBarrierException.
